                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "TLDR: A very fun and unique VR game!  It still has some kinks to work out, but I think the developer has some very interesting ideas.  It's really cool to see a VR rogue-like like this, and it has a lot of replay value and that \"let me try one more time\" vibe.  \n\n\n\nThe game has you adjust the height of the floor up to around your waist to provide a first person perspective.  This works quite well in regards to allowing you to walk around the space - but it does make you feel a bit bigger than normal.  If you approach the game knowing this, I feel that most of the negative reviews are not much of a concern.\n  \nThe weapon collision against the green block and floor of the game, which makes you drop your weapon, can be a bit frustrating.  It's not a perfectly eloquent solution to preventing players from \"cheesing\" the game, but I can see why it had to be applied.  \n\n***UPDATE***\n\nWow, you completely fixed the green block item drop thing in a way that I feel is very fair.  Making your weapon rubber band back is awesome!  This game feels even better now!  Great job!\n\nThere are also issues with going into a room and being attacked before you can react, if enemies get really close up on you, it can be hard to realize you should look down at your feet to see them due to perspective.  Hopefully as the dev continues to work on this title, the few niggles I have will be ironed out.  \n\nOverall I am giving this game a positive review. Why?  Wasn't this review a list of things wrong with the game?  I'll tell you: It's really neat to play, it's challenging, and it has more replay value than some of the more demo-ish titles on the vive.  It really feels like a complete \"game\" in many ways- though I still feel that the dev should have put this as Early Access and continued to hone it into a finer blade!  \n\nI'll put it this way, if the game was a 2d top down Gungeon / Isaac / etc. it would be getting substantially  higher reviews - but those games have been done over and over.  They are great, I absolutely love them, but this is something truly unique.  The challenges of locomotion and perspective in VR are definitely there, and this dev has some serious cajones for trying this idea!  We need to support devs that attempt creative and unique approaches to VR and there really is no single game on the vive that is like this one.   That doesn't mean it's perfect, but it earned my money by delivering something I've never experienced before.\n\nJust a side note to the dev: I know it can be discouraging to read negative reviews on your game.  Don't make the reviews constitute whether you should keep working on your game.  KEEP WORKING ON THIS.  You have something really unique here, and if you keep experimenting with the system you have I'm confident you'll create something even more special.  Personally though, I would lower the price a bit.  The \"VR tax\" as I see it can be a bit counter productive.  This game really should be on early access for 10 - 15. It's none of my business to determine price, and I'm happy with the product for what I paid (16.99 during the sale I believe), and I would have gladly paid 20, but just consider it if you're trying to make bigger sales and garner more reviews.  Sometimes in a new market you have to make your game \"a steal\" to stand out - as much as it sucks for all the time and hard work you put it into it.  As it stands only 12 reviews isnt going to show much, and that's quite a few for how limited the Vive market is at the moment.  I know this isn't really any of my business, it's yours, but I really want to see this game blossom even more into the must have title I think it deserves to be with the right polish.  Thanks for making this game! \n\nAnd thanks for the last update, which has made the game even more fun!",
